    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form",
    "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-20x-2y=-47?$",
    "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-20x-2y & =-47 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-20x-{y}^{2}-2y & =-47 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-10x]-[{y}^{2}+2y] & =-47 \\\\ 2[(x-5)^{2}-25]-[(y+1)^{2}-1] & =-47 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-50-(y+1)^{2}+1 & =-47 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =-47+50-1 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(5,-1).$",
